+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get the 4 percent tax rate on a second home in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina gives the 4 percent rate only to the home your household lives in. A second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value and also pays the school operating tax."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I rent out my second home in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, in some places. North Myrtle Beach allows nightly rentals with a city license. The City of Myrtle Beach and Surfside Beach allow them only in some zones. A second-home loan also expects you to live in it part of the year and keep control of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do my guests pay lodging tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, on any stay under 90 days: about 11 to 13 percent, depending on the town. A stay of 90 days or more in a row is not taxed as lodging."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if I rent it for less than two weeks a year?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you also use it yourself for more than 14 days, the IRS does not count the rent as income, and you deduct no rental costs."}}]}</script>
