"><text class="f">5 MILES</text></g></g><g transform="translate(343 21)"><g class="k"><path d="M0-12 5 2 0-1-5 2z" fill="#1c2028"/><text class="f" y="15" text-anchor="middle">N</text></g></g><g transform="translate(88.4 57)"><g class="k"><text class="T" x="0" y="3.7" text-anchor="middle"><tspan x="0">CONWAY</tspan></text></g></g><text class="wc"><textPath href="#c3r-second-home-beach-towns--w0" startOffset="50%" text-anchor="middle">Intracoastal Waterway</textPath></text><g transform="translate(232 200)"><g class="k"><text class="o" x="0" y="5.1" text-anchor="middle">Atlantic Ocean</text></g></g><g transform="translate(126.5 189.7)"><g class="k"><rect x="-15.9" y="-8" width="31.9" height="16" rx="3" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><text class="r" y="4" text-anchor="middle">544</text></g></g><g transform="translate(62.2 310.4)"><g class="k"><path d="M-10.7 -8H10.7V1.2C10.7 6.4 4.8 8 0 9.6C-4.8 8 -10.7 6.4 -10.7 1.2z" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><text class="r" y="3.1" text-anchor="middle">17</text></g></g><g transform="translate(93.2 213)"><g class="k"><rect x="-15.9" y="-8" width="31.9" height="16" rx="3" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><text class="r" y="4" text-anchor="middle">707</text></g></g><g transform="translate(125.8 136.7)"><g class="k"><rect x="-10.7" y="-8" width="21.5" height="16" rx="3" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><text class="r" y="4" text-anchor="middle">31</text></g></g><g><path d="M119.4 246.3L124.3 251.2" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="1.1"/><circle cx="119.4" cy="246.3" r="2.4" fill="#1c2028"/><g transform="translate(316 66)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="l" x="-15" y="5.3" text-anchor="end"><tspan x="-15">North Myrtle Beach</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(193.7 160.3)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Myrtle Beach</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(132.7 224.4)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Surfside Beach</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(124.3 251.2)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Garden City</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(97.5 260.9)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="l" x="0" y="30" text-anchor="middle"><tspan x="0">Murrells Inlet</tspan></text></g></g><g transform="translate(44 351.4)"><g class="k"><circle r="11" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#91592b" stroke-width="1.6"/><text class="nm" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text class="l" x="15" y="5.3"><tspan x="15">Pawleys Island</tspan></text></g></g></g></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">Map of the six places on this page, from North Myrtle Beach south to Pawleys Island. Tap a name to read about it. Map data: U.S. Census Bureau TIGER/Line; Horry County GIS.</figcaption></figure></div><style>main#main .detail-hero .detail-sub{display:none}</style></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="north-myrtle-beach"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="1. North Myrtle Beach">1. North Myrtle Beach: a dock behind the house, the shag on Main Street</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Cherry Grove, at the north end of North Myrtle Beach, the streets from 32nd Avenue North to the end of Cherry Grove back onto canals with boat docks. The canals run out to Hog Inlet and the marsh. Cherry Grove was an island until its inlet filled in around 1950.</p><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/no-hoa/cherry-grove-canals-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/no-hoa/cherry-grove-canals-from-the-ocean-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/no-hoa/cherry-grove-canals-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Rows of houses on long canals with boat docks, running back from the beach to a wide green marsh at Cherry Grove, seen from a plane over the ocean" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Cherry Grove from over the ocean: houses on the canals, many with their own docks, and the marsh behind.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="second-home-north-myrtle-beach" data-name="North Myrtle Beach" data-head="Homes for sale from Ocean Drive to Cherry Grove"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A few miles south, Ocean Drive is where the shag began. In the 1940s, visitors came to dance at the beach music clubs on Main Street and Ocean Drive. Each spring and fall, the Society of Stranders brings its shag festivals back to the same streets.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Myrtle Beach has 9 miles of beach. A nightly rental here needs only a city business license, renewed each year.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The canals fill with mud over time. Owners of lots on them in east Cherry Grove pay up to $2,400 a year toward digging them out, through a district set to last 10 years. The city has also held public meetings on new rental rules since 2024, with nothing adopted yet.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes in North Myrtle Beach usually sell for about $395,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="myrtle-beach-oceanfront"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="2. Myrtle Beach oceanfront">2. Myrtle Beach oceanfront: condos on the boardwalk</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Myrtle Beach Boardwalk runs 1.3 miles along the ocean, from Pier 14 to 16th Avenue North. From a condo on this stretch, you walk out to the beach, the piers and the restaurants along the boardwalk.</p><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/55-plus/del-webb-grande-dunes/myrtle-beach-beach-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/55-plus/del-webb-grande-dunes/myrtle-beach-beach-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/55-plus/del-webb-grande-dunes/myrtle-beach-beach-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="The wide sand beach, the dunes and the ocean in Myrtle Beach, seen from a high floor" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: 28bytes. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: 28bytes</a></span></div><figcaption>The wide beach and the dunes in Myrtle Beach, seen from a high floor of an oceanfront building.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="second-home-myrtle-beach-oceanfront" data-name="Myrtle Beach oceanfront" data-head="Condos for sale near the boardwalk"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The city took its name from the wax myrtle, a shrub that grows wild here. The name won a contest in 1900, and Edgewater came second. South of the towers, Myrtle Beach State Park keeps about a mile of beach with no buildings on it. It opened in 1936 as the first park in the state system.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The city allows nightly rentals only where the zoning allows them, and most of its neighborhoods of houses are zoned against them. The fine is up to $500 for each offense.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some oceanfront buildings run like hotels, with a registration desk and daily rentals. Those buildings are harder to finance, as the <a href="#loan" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">loan section</a> explains.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes across the City of Myrtle Beach usually sell for about $325,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="surfside-beach"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="3. Surfside Beach">3. Surfside Beach: a town pier and streets of beach houses</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Surfside Beach owns its fishing pier. It is concrete and 814 feet long. Walking on it is free, and a day of fishing costs $14, with the state fishing license included.</p><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/no-hoa/surfside-beach-houses-from-the-sand-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/no-hoa/surfside-beach-houses-from-the-sand-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/no-hoa/surfside-beach-houses-from-the-sand-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="A row of two- and three-story beach houses in blue, yellow and gray behind low dunes, seen across a wide sand beach under a blue sky at Surfside Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: BullDawg2021. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: BullDawg2021</a></span></div><figcaption>Beach houses behind the low dunes on the ocean side of Surfside Beach.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="surfside-beach" data-name="Surfside Beach" data-head="Homes for sale near the Surfside Beach pier"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Much of the town was once The Ark Plantation. In the 1920s George J. Holiday named it Floral Beach, after his wife Flora and his daughter Floramay. It became Surfside Beach in 1952. When Hurricane Hazel came in 1954, it destroyed 18 of the 65 houses on the beach.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The town has 36 beach access points and 12 parking lots near the beach. Parking by the pier costs $4 an hour.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Stays under 30 days are allowed in only two of the town's zoning districts, called R3 and C3. The town's single-family neighborhoods do not allow them, and we tell you which district a house is in.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes in Surfside Beach usually sell for about $360,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="garden-city-beach"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="4. Garden City Beach">4. Garden City Beach: the ocean on one side, the inlet on the other</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Garden City Beach is a strip of land between the ocean and the marsh of Murrells Inlet. Houses on the marsh side have docks on the tidal creeks.</p><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/no-hoa/garden-city-beach-and-marsh-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/no-hoa/garden-city-beach-and-marsh-from-the-ocean-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/no-hoa/garden-city-beach-and-marsh-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Beach houses in rows between the ocean beach and a wide marsh with tidal creeks and boat docks at Garden City Beach, seen from a plane over the ocean" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Garden City Beach from over the ocean: rows of houses between the beach and the marsh, with docks on the creeks behind.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="second-home-garden-city-beach" data-name="Garden City Beach" data-head="Homes for sale near the Garden City pier"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the Fourth of July, the Murrells Inlet boat parade starts from the point of Garden City. People watch it from the MarshWalk across the water.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Garden City is not a town. It is split between two counties, and its south end is in Georgetown County. On the Horry County side you may rent by the night, unless the house's HOA forbids it, and many houses there have <a href="/buyers/no-hoa-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">no HOA</a>. We read the HOA's rules, and check the zoning on the Georgetown County side, before you make an offer.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes in the 29576 ZIP code, which covers Garden City and Murrells Inlet, usually sell for about $400,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="murrells-inlet"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="5. Murrells Inlet">5. Murrells Inlet: seafood on the boardwalk, boats in the creek</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Murrells Inlet began as a fishing village on a sheltered inlet. Its MarshWalk, a half-mile boardwalk along the water, has seafood restaurants and the docks for fishing trips.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="second-home-murrells-inlet" data-name="Murrells Inlet" data-head="Homes for sale near the MarshWalk"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Huntington Beach State Park, beside the inlet, has 3 miles of beach. In the park is Atalaya, the Moorish-style winter home of Archer and Anna Hyatt Huntington, now a National Historic Landmark. Brookgreen Gardens, across the highway, has one of the country's largest collections of American statues of people and animals.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Murrells Inlet is not a town either. On the Horry County side, the county has no rule on nightly stays, so only an HOA can forbid them.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The waterway along the inlet is in the highest-risk flood zone on the coast, where storms bring waves. A home there with a mortgage needs flood insurance, and we get a flood quote for any home you like.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes in Murrells Inlet usually sell for about $400,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="pawleys-island"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="6. Pawleys Island">6. Pawleys Island: summer houses since the 1700s</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rice planters built summer houses on Pawleys Island in the early 1700s, to get away from the rivers in summer, so it is one of the oldest beach resorts in America. Its historic district, in the middle of the island, has houses from about 1780 to 1866.</p><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-600w.webp 600w, /images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="900" alt="A wooden walkway over green dunes leading to the ocean on Pawleys Island" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Phoebe. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: Phoebe</a></span></div><figcaption>A wooden walkway over the dunes to the ocean on Pawleys Island.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="second-home-pawleys-island" data-name="Pawleys Island" data-head="Homes for sale on and near Pawleys Island"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The town incorporated in 1985 to keep out a large hotel, and it forbids commercial businesses. Apart from two historic inns, the island has no commercial buildings. The Pawleys Island rope hammock was first made here. Pawleys Creek, the tidal creek behind the island's salt marsh, is a wildlife sanctuary with no hunting.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Pawleys Island allows rentals, and the town requires no business license to rent a home out.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Parts of the island flood when heavy rain comes with a high tide, including near both causeways. The streets are narrow, with 20 feet of pavement or less.</p><p class="c3-place-price">Homes with a Pawleys Island address, on the island and the mainland side, usually sell for about $550,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="renting"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Renting it">Can you rent it out when you are not there?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, in most of these places. Each town makes its own rule, an HOA can forbid what the town allows, and your loan has its own limits.</p><div class="c3s-t"><table><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Nightly and weekly rentals</th><th>What we check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>City of Myrtle Beach</td><td>Only where the zoning allows them. Most neighborhoods of houses do not.</td><td>The unit's zoning</td></tr><tr><td>North Myrtle Beach</td><td>Allowed with a city business license, renewed each year.</td><td>New rules, if the city adopts them</td></tr><tr><td>Surfside Beach</td><td>Only in the R3 and C3 zoning districts.</td><td>The house's district</td></tr><tr><td>Horry County outside the cities, including Garden City and Murrells Inlet</td><td>Allowed. The county has no rule.</td><td>The HOA's rules, if there is an HOA</td></tr><tr><td>Pawleys Island</td><td>Allowed. No town business license is needed.</td><td>The house's own papers</td></tr><tr><td>Georgetown County outside the towns</td><td>A house rented for 30 days or less is allowed in the resort districts.</td><td>The zoning</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="c3s-t"><table><thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>What a nightly rental took in, past year</th><th>Homes usually sell for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North Myrtle Beach</td><td>About $29,000</td><td>About $395,000</td></tr><tr><td>City of Myrtle Beach, all of it</td><td>About $21,000</td><td>About $325,000</td></tr><tr><td>Surfside Beach</td><td>About $35,000</td><td>About $360,000</td></tr><tr><td>Garden City Beach</td><td>About $34,000</td><td>About $400,000 (ZIP 29576)</td></tr><tr><td>Murrells Inlet</td><td>About $38,000</td><td>About $400,000</td></tr><tr><td>Pawleys Island</td><td>About $28,000</td><td>About $550,000 (the whole Pawleys Island address)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rental figures are averages for the past year, before the manager, cleaning, taxes and the HOA fee. Your own place can earn more or less, depending on the home, its view and how it is run.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the numbers for one home?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the listing. We check whether you may rent it by the night, read its HOA rules, and give you its tax bill as a second home.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/?topic=second-home">Send us the home</a></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Guests pay lodging tax on any stay under 90 days: about 13 percent in the City of Myrtle Beach, about 12 percent elsewhere in Horry County, and about 11 percent on Pawleys Island. You or your rental manager collect it from guests and send it in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The IRS has a rule for a home you both use and rent. Use it yourself for more than 14 days a year, or more than 10 percent of the days it is rented, whichever is more, and the IRS treats it as a home. Then rent from fewer than 15 days a year is tax-free, and your rental deductions cannot be more than the rent. Our <a href="/invest/14-day-rule/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">14-day rule</a> page has examples.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="loan"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Which loan">Is it a second home or an investment property?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Decide this before you apply for a loan. Most lenders follow the rules of Fannie Mae, which buys home loans from lenders. Fannie Mae calls a home a second home only when:</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">you live in it yourself for part of the year;</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">it is one home, not a duplex;</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">it can be lived in all year;</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">no agreement gives a management company control over who stays there;</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">the rent from it is not used to qualify you for the loan.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some rent is allowed. If the lender sees rental income, the loan can still be a second-home loan, as long as the rent did not help you qualify and every other rule is met. A home you will not live in is an investment property, and lenders price that loan differently.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fannie Mae does not buy loans in a condo building run like a hotel, with a rental pool or daily rentals, so many lenders will not finance a unit there. Other loans exist for those buildings: see <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">loans for hotel-style condos</a>. Our lending partner can lay out both kinds of loan for the home you choose.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="cost"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Yearly cost">What does a second home cost to hold each year?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina taxes a second home on 6 percent of its value, and the home you live in on 4 percent. A home you live in also pays no school operating tax, the largest part of the bill. Together, the two rules make a second home's bill three to four times larger.</p><div class="c3s-t"><table><thead><tr><th>A $400,000 home, 2026</th><th>If you lived in it</th><th>As a second home</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>City of Myrtle Beach</td><td>About $1,615 a year</td><td>About $6,110 a year</td></tr><tr><td>Horry County outside the cities</td><td>About $1,470 a year</td><td>About $4,820 a year</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In North Myrtle Beach and Surfside Beach, the bill on a $400,000 second home falls between these two. Every town's bill is in our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Insurance is the other large cost, and the condo and beach house table below shows who insures what. A federal flood policy has a $250 yearly surcharge when the home is no one's main home. For a main home, the surcharge is $25. Renting the home out will likely raise the premium. What each policy costs here is on our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance</a> page.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We get the insurance quotes and the HOA fee for any home you like before you make an offer, so you know the whole yearly cost.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="condo-or-house"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo or house">Should you buy a condo or a beach house?</h2><div class="c3s-t"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>A condo</th><th>A beach house</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The building</td><td>The association insures it and keeps up the outside. The cost is in the monthly fee.</td><td>You insure it against wind and often flood, and the roof is yours.</td></tr><tr><td>Your own policy</td><td>Covers the inside of the unit and your things.</td><td>Covers the whole house.</td></tr><tr><td>Big repairs</td><td>An older building can send every owner a one-time bill, called a special assessment.</td><td>You pay for them when they come.</td></tr><tr><td>The loan</td><td>Harder in a building run like a hotel (see <a href="#loan" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the loan</a>).</td><td>Any second-home or investment loan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read a building's budget and its <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessments</a> before you make an offer on a condo.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="second-home-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Second home FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I get the 4 percent tax rate on a second home in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. South Carolina gives the 4 percent rate only to the home your household lives in, and the owner certifies that no one in the household is a legal resident of another state. A second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I use a rental manager with a second-home loan?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, as long as no agreement gives the manager control over who stays in the home and when. The lender also cannot count the rent toward qualifying you for a second-home loan.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who looks after a second home while I am away?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A local property manager can check on the home, handle repairs and, if you rent it, run the bookings. Chapter3 sells homes and does not manage them, and our property management page explains what managers here charge.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What about hurricane season?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Atlantic hurricane season runs from June 1 to November 30. A beach house needs a wind policy and usually flood coverage, and a condo owner relies on the building's master policy. We check the flood zone of any home before you make an offer.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.1-01/occupancy-types" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae Selling Guide, occupancy types</a>, <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/departments/planning_and_zoning_department.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">City of Myrtle Beach zoning</a>, <a href="https://www.nmb.us/833/Short-term-Rentals" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Myrtle Beach short-term rentals</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43-220</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/kufln4qp/tax-levy-2026_2.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County 2026 tax levy</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/sites/dor/files/forms/ST575.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Department of Revenue, lodging tax rates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 527</a>. Also the towns' own pages, the Surfside and Georgetown County zoning codes, SC State Parks, AirROI's rental data and Zillow's home value index for September 2026 (condos and houses together). Tax figures are estimates from 2026 tax rates. Not legal or tax advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Cherry Grove from over the ocean: houses on the canals, many with their own docks, and the marsh behind. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"