-## Frequently asked questions
+## Second home FAQ
 
-### How much down payment does a second home require?
-Conventional second-home loans require a larger down payment than a primary residence, and putting more down generally buys better pricing. Second-home rates run slightly above primary-residence rates.
+### Can I get the 4 percent tax rate on a second home in South Carolina?
+No. South Carolina gives the 4 percent rate only to the home your household lives in, and the owner certifies that no one in the household is a legal resident of another state. A second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value.
 
-### How is a second home taxed in Horry County?
-Property that is not your primary residence is assessed at 6 percent instead of the 4 percent owner-occupied rate, so carrying costs are higher than the same home held as a primary residence. The full math, with a calculator: Horry County property taxes .
+### Can I use a rental manager with a second-home loan?
+Yes, as long as no agreement gives the manager control over who stays in the home and when. The lender also cannot count the rent toward qualifying you for a second-home loan.
 
-### Can I rent out my second home?
-Lender guidelines require a second home to be available for your personal use, with some rental activity allowed. Heavy rental use pushes the loan into investment-property territory with different rates and down payment rules.
+### Who looks after a second home while I am away?
+A local property manager can check on the home, handle repairs and, if you rent it, run the bookings. Chapter3 sells homes and does not manage them, and our property management page explains what managers here charge.
 
-### Can I rent out my second home in Myrtle Beach?
-Only if your financing allows it. Second-home mortgages carry occupancy rules, and renting the home out can violate the loan terms. If rental income is part of your plan, use an investment loan instead, such as a DSCR loan that qualifies on the property's rent. Decide before you finance, not after.
+### What about hurricane season?
+The Atlantic hurricane season runs from June 1 to November 30. A beach house needs a wind policy and usually flood coverage, and a condo owner relies on the building's master policy. We check the flood zone of any home before you make an offer.
